1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,275
Welcome to Module Twenty-Eight.

2
00:00:02,275 --> 00:00:04,337
Yesterday was imitation.

3
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:05,750
Copy the parent.

4
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:08,062
Today is selection on behaviour.

5
00:00:08,062 --> 00:00:14,537
An individual tries something, the environment scores the outcome, and behaviours that pay off become more common.

6
00:00:14,537 --> 00:00:20,250
No teacher needed, which means behaviour can go beyond anything a teacher ever showed.

7
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:22,262
That's reinforcement learning.

8
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:24,325
The model is called the policy.

9
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:30,012
It plays out a whole episode, which we call a rollout: act, observe, act again.

10
00:00:30,012 --> 00:00:34,275
At the end, a reward, one number, says how well it went.

11
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:39,837
Then the policy is nudged so that actions from better-than-expected rollouts become more likely.

12
00:00:39,837 --> 00:00:42,512
Better than expected is the key phrase.

13
00:00:42,512 --> 00:00:44,662
A lecture in this pack shows why.

14
00:00:44,662 --> 00:00:51,350
Raw rewards are noisy, so you compare each rollout with a baseline, a sense of what the policy usually gets.

15
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:55,125
One popular method gets that baseline very simply.

16
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:59,912
It plays the same task about eight times and uses the average reward of the group.

17
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:02,800
Rollouts above the average are reinforced.

18
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,200
Rollouts below it are discouraged.

19
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,237
There's a catch you'll recognise from selection in small populations.

20
00:01:09,237 --> 00:01:13,650
If every rollout in the group fails, there's nothing to select between.

21
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,412
No variation, no response.

22
00:01:16,412 --> 00:01:21,337
Reinforcement learning can only amplify what the model can already sometimes do.

23
00:01:21,337 --> 00:01:26,650
So people start with easier tasks, or give hints, or teach by imitation first.

24
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:28,362
And now the dark side.

25
00:01:28,362 --> 00:01:30,837
Selection acts on whatever is measured.

26
00:01:30,837 --> 00:01:36,050
Select hens for eggs per cage, and you may select the hen that bullies her neighbours.

27
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:41,725
In machine learning this is called reward hacking, and it's Goodhart's law with a training loop attached.

28
00:01:41,725 --> 00:01:48,037
The pack includes an audit of coding agents that looked successful but had often just looked up the known fix.

29
00:01:48,037 --> 00:01:51,675
When the shortcuts were blocked, their scores dropped sharply.

30
00:01:51,675 --> 00:01:58,387
One research team puts the lesson plainly: verification has to keep evolving alongside the model it checks.

31
00:01:58,387 --> 00:02:01,825
That's the Red Queen again, now inside training.

32
00:02:01,825 --> 00:02:05,950
In the lab, your coding agent will build the guessing game from the lecture.

33
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:10,325
A hidden number from one to sixteen, four guesses, higher or lower.

34
00:02:10,325 --> 00:02:13,000
A tiny policy will learn to play it.

35
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,262
You'll compare learning with and without a baseline.

36
00:02:16,262 --> 00:02:24,712
Then you'll plant a bug in the reward checker, so that one illegal guess also counts as correct, and you'll watch the reward climb while the real success rate falls.

37
00:02:24,712 --> 00:02:34,912
Finally, you'll package the small decision model from the last two modules into one function your capstone can call, with a gate that only lets it act if it passes your held-out test.

38
00:02:34,912 --> 00:02:36,662
Start with Part A.

39
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:40,900
Before you run anything, predict what the cheating policy's curves will look like.

